{"name":"Makeup sponge (beauty blender)","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Silicone_makeup_sponge_being_held_by_a_person_in_a_well-lit_indoor_setting.jpg/960px-Silicone_makeup_sponge_being_held_by_a_person_in_a_well-lit_indoor_setting.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silicone_makeup_sponge_being_held_by_a_person_in_a_well-lit_indoor_setting.jpg","caption":"Silicone makeup sponge being held by a person in a well-lit indoor setting","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A makeup sponge is a reusable foam tool, usually egg-shaped and edgeless, used to press and blend liquid, cream and powder makeup onto skin. The Beautyblender, introduced in the early 2000s, made the form widely popular.","It helps looks by making base makeup look smoother and less patchy, which evens out the appearance of skin tone and blemishes."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Users dampen the sponge so it expands, then bounce or dab product onto the face rather than swiping. The pointed tip reaches around the eyes and nose. Sponges are washed between uses and replaced periodically."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Wet sponges can harbor bacteria if not cleaned and dried, so makeup guides recommend washing and not sharing them. Latex-free polyurethane foam is generally used, which suits people with latex sensitivity."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Silicone_makeup_sponge_being_held_by_a_person_in_a_well-lit_indoor_setting.jpg/960px-Silicone_makeup_sponge_being_held_by_a_person_in_a_well-lit_indoor_setting.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Silicone_makeup_sponge_being_held_by_a_person_in_a_well-lit_indoor_setting.jpg?utm_source=co